WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.990
Two teachers asked forty pupils each

2
00:00:02.990 --> 00:00:04.730
how long they spend on homework.

3
00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:07.690
One found fifty minutes and

4
00:00:07.690 --> 00:00:08.970
the other found sixty.

5
00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:11.180
Was one of them wrong?

6
00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:15.510
Today: why a sample never gives exactly

7
00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:18.110
the right answer, and how wrong it might

8
00:00:18.110 --> 00:00:18.390
be.

9
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:22.060
The population is everyone we care

10
00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:24.460
about, say all eight hundred pupils.

11
00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:27.930
A sample is the group we actually ask.

12
00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:30.400
We use the sample to estimate

13
00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:32.140
something about the whole population.

14
00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:35.410
Pick forty pupils at random

15
00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:37.230
and you get one average.

16
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.060
Pick another forty and you get a

17
00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:41.080
slightly different one.

18
00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:43.010
Neither teacher was wrong,

19
00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:45.350
because every random sample

20
00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:46.870
wobbles around the true value.

21
00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:50.810
We took twenty random samples from the

22
00:00:50.810 --> 00:00:53.250
school records and plotted their averages.

23
00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:56.760
Most land between fifty and sixty minutes,

24
00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:58.820
around the true value of fifty five.

25
00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:03.290
So we report a range, not one number.

26
00:01:03.950 --> 00:01:06.110
We say fifty five minutes,

27
00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:07.530
plus or minus five.

28
00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:10.860
A bigger sample shrinks the margin, but

29
00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:13.660
four times as many pupils only halves it.

30
00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:16.680
A big sample can still be

31
00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:18.720
wrong if it is not random.

32
00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:22.390
If we only ask pupils who stay after

33
00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:25.470
school, we miss those who leave early to

34
00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:25.770
work.

35
00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:29.140
Always ask who was left out.

36
00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:31.060
Quick check!

37
00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:34.850
Two random samples of forty pupils

38
00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:38.370
give fifty and sixty minutes.

39
00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:41.260
What is the best explanation?

40
00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:45.290
Random samples vary, so both

41
00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:46.170
answers are reasonable.

42
00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:49.240
Together they suggest the true value

43
00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:51.060
is somewhere near fifty five.

44
00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:53.930
A range tells that story honestly.

45
00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:57.480
In the app, open the sampling lab.

46
00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:00.880
Draw many samples, then make each

47
00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:02.980
sample bigger, and watch the

48
00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:04.980
averages crowd closer together.

49
00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:06.710
Your turn.

50
00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:09.940
We want a smaller margin of error.

51
00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:11.400
What should we do?

52
00:02:12.710 --> 00:02:14.690
Ask more pupils, chosen at random.

53
00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:18.590
Remember: samples wobble, so report a

54
00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:21.270
range, and check who your sample left out.

55
00:02:22.490 --> 00:02:25.050
Try it in class tomorrow: put sixty

56
00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:27.290
numbered cards in a bag, and let each

57
00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:29.230
group draw ten and find the average.

58
00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:31.950
Write every group's average on the board

59
00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:34.710
and ask: why are they all different?
